GNOME Logs — Ruby GTK4 port
===========================

A log viewer for the systemd journal, ported from the original C/GTK4
implementation to Ruby using the ruby-gnome gtk4 and adwaita bindings.

https://apps.gnome.org/Logs/


Running
-------

    nix run .

or, from a source checkout:

    nix develop
    ruby -Ilib bin/gnome-logs

The flake pins every gem through gemset.nix, so no network access or `bundle
install` is needed. To regenerate it after changing the Gemfile:

    nix develop .#bundle -c bundle lock
    nix develop .#bundle -c bundix


Requirements
------------

GTK 4, libadwaita, and a `journalctl` on PATH. The package prefers the host's
journalctl (a journal viewer should match the host's journald) and falls back
to the systemd in the closure.


What changed from the C version
-------------------------------

The port keeps the behaviour and drops the machinery that only existed to work
around C and libsystemd constraints.

  * Journal access goes through `journalctl --output=json` rather than
    libsystemd, because Ruby has no libsystemd binding. journalctl's match
    semantics (OR within a field, AND across fields) are the same ones
    gl_query_to_string built by hand, so the category filters translate
    directly.

  * GlJournalModel is gone. It existed to page a journal cursor through idle
    callbacks; one batch read with a row cap replaces roughly 1400 lines.

  * The row compression system (grouping repeated adjacent entries behind an
    expander) is not ported. Entries that share a timestamp still collapse
    their time column, which is the part of it users actually see.

  * The message catalog is dumped once and parsed into subject / defined-by /
    support / documentation, instead of picking each entry apart with strtok.

  * Detail rows are always shown rather than hidden when their field is empty,
    so the popover does not resize as you move between entries.

  * Priorities render as names (Warning, Error…) instead of the raw number the
    C version left as a TODO.

  * The search popover uses Gtk::DropDown rather than the deprecated
    GtkTreeView, and a custom range covers whole days instead of pairing each
    calendar with hour and minute spin buttons.

  * The Alerts, Starred, Updates and Usage categories are not ported: they are
    hidden, unimplemented rows upstream.

  * The sort-order GSetting is a string with explicit choices rather than an
    enum generated from the C headers.


Layout
------

    bin/gnome-logs             source-checkout entry point
    lib/gnome_logs/main.rb     installed entry point
    lib/gnome_logs/
      application.rb           app actions, accelerators, about dialog
      window.rb                window, warning bar, export
      event_toolbar.rb         header bar, boot selector, primary menu
      event_view_list.rb       sidebar + log list, reload pipeline
      event_view_row.rb        one log row
      event_view_detail.rb     the detail popover
      category_list.rb         sidebar
      search_popover.rb        search field / type / range options
      shortcuts_window.rb      keyboard shortcuts window
      journal.rb               journalctl backend
      journal_entry.rb         one entry
      query.rb                 what to read and how to filter it
      category.rb              the six categories
      catalog.rb               systemd message catalog
      settings.rb              org.gnome.Logs GSettings


Style
-----

The Ruby follows the declarative memoized-widget style: every widget is a
memoized method, all configuration lives in that method's tap block, assembly
happens in a single build method whose nesting mirrors the widget tree, and
the app starts with one expression. `rubocop` is clean; .rubocop.yml documents
which defaults the style deliberately overrides.
